{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target argues for the large service but fails to address the problem's core requirement: distinguishing failure modes that are truly independent from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. Its mechanism describes cascading failures in decentralized networks, but it never engages the correlated-failure evidence question at all. Worse, it implicitly assumes the large service's internal redundancy is genuinely independent while asserting decentralized failures are correlated via propagation — yet it provides no evidentiary criterion to distinguish real independence from merely-appearing-uncorrelated internal redundancy (e.g., the large service's redundant components sharing a common codebase, common config, or common deployment that creates a hidden common-cause failure). The counterconditions only specify observation-triggered escapes but give no discriminating evidence test. By the problem's own standard, an argument that takes a side on resilience without identifying the distinguishing evidence for independence-vs-apparent-independence is incomplete.","counterexample":["A single large internally-redundant service and a decentralized network could both pass the target's described criteria (centralized circuit-breaking vs. loosely-coupled async communication), yet still share a hidden common cause — e.g., identical TLS library version across all redundant components — making the target's resilience comparison undecidable without the evidence test the problem demands."],"target":"5256fc7a4ff5c37de5c351521bb5c356f0a7f62b676cbe11a5a58af56c46acad"}]}